2-T3 chow, but I could still form T2-T3-T4 chow if I keep T2, T3, T4. That might be acceptable.  \n\nBut considering the pair of T7, I might aim for a hand with chows in T suit and a pair of T7, plus the exposed B chow. That would be a half-flush possibly (only T suit and honors? but I have B chow exposed, so not pure).  \n\nGiven all, I think discarding an honor is safer and preserves my sequences. Between F4 and J3, F4 is my seat wind, which could be worth points if I get a pair, but it's a single. J3 is a dragon, also single. Both are not useful now. I'll choose F4 because it's less likely to be needed by opponents? Actually, dragons are often coveted for points. But from history, no one has shown interest in dragons much. J3 was discarded by across. F4 hasn't been discarded. I'll go with F4.  \n\nReason: I choose to discard F4 (North Wind) because it is a single honor tile that does not contribute to my hand's sequences or pairs, and it is relatively safe given that no opponents have shown strong interest in North Wind. This preserves my T suit sequences and pairs, maintaining flexibility for potential chows and a pair wait.  \n\nAnswer: Play F4"}
